SwirlRun — userspace Win32 PE loader for SwirlOS (r18)
======================================================

SwirlRun runs real Windows x64 executables (PE32+) on SwirlOS with
ZERO kernel support for .exe files. The kernel knows nothing about
PE; SwirlRun is an ordinary ring-3 ELF (like Wine on Linux).

  kernel: no exe logic, no PE parsing, no compat shims.
          One new GENERIC syscall (79: set ring-3 TLS/GS base).
  userspace: everything else.

What runs today
---------------
  /system/apps/doom.exe   - a real Windows PE built with mingw-w64:
                            the doomgeneric core + a Win32/GDI backend
                            (RegisterClassExA, CreateWindowExA,
                            CreateDIBSection, StretchDIBits, PeekMessage,
                            timeGetTime). Plays the shareware demo from
                            C:\DOOM\doom1.wad (= /home/user/DOOM).
  /system/apps/hello.exe  - console fixture: WriteFile/printf/heap/sin().

Boot one with swirl.conf:
    [test] exe = doom.exe        (or hello.exe; 0 = off - DOOM autostart
                                  is OFF by default; launch games from
                                  Files or the desktop instead)

32-bit (i386 PE32) games run through the w32 layer (r23): kernel32,
user32, gdi32, shell32, version, shlwapi, imm32, winmm, msvcrt, ddraw,
dinput, dsound. See Games/README.txt in the home folder.

How it works
------------
swirlrun.elf (slot 3, 20 MB static arena) does:

  1. resolve the spawn argument to an exe (Win32-style path mapping:
     "C:\..." -> /home/user/..., "D:\..." -> /system/..., per-component
     case-insensitive lookup like Wine's realpath),
  2. read the whole PE, parse DOS/NT headers (PE32+ x64 only; 32-bit
     PE and MZ/DOS are rejected with a clear message),
  3. map sections into the arena, apply DIR64 base relocations,
  4. bind every IAT entry against builtin "DLLs" implemented in this
     same process with the Microsoft x64 calling convention
     (gcc ms_abi):
        kernel32.dll  memory/files/console/process/timing/sync/TLS
        msvcrt.dll    heap/stdio(printf family w/ ms varargs)/
                      string/ctype/math/qsort/CRT startup
        user32.dll    classes/windows/messages (kernel WM events
                      translated into real WM_KEYDOWN/MOUSEMOVE/etc),
        gdi32.dll     CreateDIBSection + StretchDIBits blits into the
                      WM window canvas + present
        winmm.dll     timeGetTime (waveOut is a silent stub: SwirlOS
                      has no audio mixer yet)
        ntdll/shell32/advapi32  minimal stub tier
     Unknown functions in known DLLs bind to a warn-once stub (Wine
     policy); unknown DLLs fail the load loudly,
  5. build a TEB + PEB in the arena, anchor the TEB at gs:[0] via
     syscall 79 (SYS_TLS_SET - the arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_GS) of SwirlOS;
     the scheduler carries the base across context switches),
  6. run TLS callbacks, then jump to the entry point with RCX = PEB,
     exactly like Windows. Exit goes through kernel32!ExitProcess.

File paths: Win32 paths are mapped on every file call (CreateFileA,
fopen, FindFirstFile...). doom.exe searches C:\DOOM\doom1.wad, which
lands on /home/user/DOOM/doom1.wad - the shareware WAD ships with the
system.

The doom.exe build
------------------
Built by the Makefile with the portable mingw-w64 rootfs
(tools/mingw-rootfs, extracted from Debian packages - no root needed):

  make build/swirlrun-pe/doom.exe

Sources: the in-tree doomgeneric port (user/doom) + a new Win32
backend (user/swirlrun/pebuild/doomgeneric_win.c) + doom_main.c
(GetCommandLineA parsing, -mb 6 -iwad C:\DOOM\doom1.wad defaults).

Important build flags (DOOMPE_CFLAGS in the Makefile):
  -fno-builtin           REQUIRED. The guest CRT is SwirlRun's msvcrt;
                         GCC 14's builtin semantics for strncasecmp &
                         friends miscompile against it (observed: the
                         R_CheckTextureNumForName chain walk turned
                         into a jmp-to-self at -O2; the whole PE is
                         built at -O1 for the same reason).
  -D__USE_MINGW_ANSI_STDIO=0
                         printf resolves to msvcrt!printf (ours) rather
                         than libmingwex's private formatter.

Known limits (v1)
-----------------
  - x64 PE32+ only; i386 PE32 and MZ/DOS exes are detected and
    rejected with an explanatory message.
  - Single thread: no CreateThread (SwirlOS has no clone). Guests that
    need threads won't run; DOOM's core does not.
  - waveOut* are silent (no audio stack in SwirlOS yet).
  - No LoadLibrary of on-disk DLLs; the builtin registry is fixed.
  - C++ exceptions / SEH unwinding are stubs: guests must not throw
    (DOOM doesn't).

Debugging tips
--------------
  - Everything logs to the serial console: loader banners, each bound
    DLL, STUB warnings, load failures with reasons.
  - The kernel's exception dump gives RIP/CR2; subtract the load base
    (logged as "image loaded at 0x...") to map onto the exe.
  - Quick fixture loop: set [test] exe = hello.exe for a 5-second
    end-to-end check of loader + CRT + console.

DirectDraw (r20)
  DDRAW.DLL is a builtin: DirectDrawCreate/Ex/Clipper/EnumerateA/ExA, one
  object model for IDirectDraw/2/4/7 and IDirectDrawSurface/2/3/4/7 (later
  versions only append methods, so one vtable serves all). Software surfaces in
  8/16/24/32 bpp, palettes, colour keys, Blt (fill, stretch, key), BltFast,
  Lock/Unlock, Flip chains. The primary surface is converted to the WM window
  canvas on Flip/Blt/Unlock. Not yet: GetDC/ReleaseDC, overlays, FourCC/YUV,
  Direct3D, DirectSound, DirectInput. Layout is x64 (64-bit guests); 32-bit
  guests need the PE32 layer first.
